          "content": [
            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Prove that any two nonabelian groups of order 21 are isomorphic.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"abstract_algebra\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"Let \\\\(G\\\\) and \\\\(H\\\\) be arbitrary nonabelian groups of order \\\\(21\\\\). We show that they are isomorphic.\\n\\nFor \\\\(G\\\\), Sylow’s theorems give\\n\\\\[\\nn_7\\\\mid 3,\\\\qquad n_7\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod 7,\\n\\\\]\\nso \\\\(n_7=1\\\\). Thus the subgroup \\\\(N=\\\\langle b\\\\rangle\\\\) of order \\\\(7\\\\) is normal. By Cauchy’s theorem, choose \\\\(a\\\\in G\\\\) of order \\\\(3\\\\). Since \\\\(N\\\\cap\\\\langle a\\\\rangle=\\\\{e\\\\}\\\\), the product \\\\(N\\\\langle a\\\\rangle\\\\) has order \\\\(21\\\\), hence equals \\\\(G\\\\). Therefore every element of \\\\(G\\\\) has a unique expression \\\\(b^s a^r\\\\), where \\\\(0\\\\le s<7\\\\) and \\\\(0\\\\le r<3\\\\).\\n\\nBecause \\\\(N\\\\) is normal, conjugation by \\\\(a\\\\) induces an automorphism of \\\\(N\\\\), so\\n\\\\[\\naba^{-1}=b^i\\n\\\\]\\nfor some \\\\(i\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\ldots,6\\\\}\\\\). Since \\\\(a^3=e\\\\), applying conjugation three times gives \\\\(b=b^{i^3}\\\\), and hence\\n\\\\[\\ni^3\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod 7.\\n\\\\]\\nThe solutions are \\\\(i\\\\equiv1,2,4\\\\pmod7\\\\). If \\\\(i=1\\\\), then \\\\(a\\\\) commutes with \\\\(b\\\\); since \\\\(a,b\\\\) generate \\\\(G\\\\), this would make \\\\(G\\\\) abelian. Thus \\\\(i=2\\\\) or \\\\(i=4\\\\). If \\\\(i=4\\\\), replace \\\\(a\\\\) by \\\\(a^{-1}\\\\); conjugation by \\\\(a^{-1}\\\\) then has exponent \\\\(4^{-1}\\\\equiv2\\\\pmod7\\\\). Consequently, generators \\\\(x,y\\\\) of orders \\\\(3,7\\\\), respectively, may be chosen so that\\n\\\\[\\nxyx^{-1}=y^2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nApplying the same argument to \\\\(H\\\\), choose generators \\\\(u,v\\\\) of orders \\\\(3,7\\\\) satisfying\\n\\\\[\\nuvu^{-1}=v^2,\\n\\\\]\\nand every element of \\\\(H\\\\) is uniquely \\\\(v^s u^r\\\\).\\n\\nDefine\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi:G\\\\to H,\\\\qquad \\\\phi(y^s x^r)=v^s u^r\\n\\\\]\\nfor \\\\(0\\\\le s<7\\\\) and \\\\(0\\\\le r<3\\\\). Uniqueness of the normal forms makes \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) well-defined and bijective. Moreover,\\n\\\\[\\n(y^s x^r)(y^q x^t)=y^{s+2^r q}x^{r+t},\\n\\\\]\\nwith exponents reduced modulo \\\\(7\\\\) and \\\\(3\\\\), respectively. The identical multiplication formula holds for \\\\(v^s u^r\\\\). Hence\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi\\\\bigl((y^s x^r)(y^q x^t)\\\\bigr)\\n=v^{s+2^r q}u^{r+t}\\n=\\\\phi(y^s x^r)\\\\phi(y^q x^t).\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) is a bijective homomorphism, so \\\\(G\\\\cong H\\\\). Therefore any two nonabelian groups of order \\\\(21\\\\) are isomorphic.\"}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
