\\\phi:G\\\\to H,\\\\qquad \\\\phi(y^s x^r)=v^s u^r\\n\\\\]\\nfor \\\\(0\\\\le s<7\\\\) and \\\\(0\\\\le r<3\\\\). Uniqueness of the normal forms makes \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) well-defined and bijective. Moreover,\\n\\\\[\\n(y^s x^r)(y^q x^t)=y^{s+2^r q}x^{r+t},\\n\\\\]\\nwith exponents reduced modulo \\\\(7\\\\) and \\\\(3\\\\), respectively. The identical multiplication formula holds for \\\\(v^s u^r\\\\). Hence\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\phi\\\\bigl((y^s x^r)(y^q x^t)\\\\bigr)\\n=v^{s+2^r q}u^{r+t}\\n=\\\\phi(y^s x^r)\\\\phi(y^q x^t).\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\phi\\\\) is a bijective homomorphism, so \\\\(G\\\\cong H\\\\). Therefore any two nonabelian groups of order \\\\(21\\\\) are isomorphic.\"}",
